# LeakSanitizer suppressions (ARCHITECTURE.md §13).
#
# THIS FILE SHOULD STAY ALMOST EMPTY. loftail's own suite leaks nothing, and the one
# by-design retention — an abandoned fetcher whose thread is still running — is parked in
# a reachable static rather than leaked, precisely so that it needs no entry here
# (SourceSpool::drainRetired). An entry is justified only when the allocation is made and
# lost entirely inside a third-party library, with no loftail frame that could free it.
#
# --- libproxy, reached through Qt's system-proxy lookup ---------------------
#
# QTcpSocket::connectToHost() asks QNetworkProxyFactory::systemProxyForQuery() what proxy
# applies, which on Linux dlopens libproxy; libproxy's GObject backend allocates a manager
# and its backing lists and never frees them. Six allocations, 141 bytes, every one of
# them under px_proxy_factory_new with nothing of loftail's below it.
#
# It is not reproducible on every machine — it appears on the ubuntu-24.04 runner and not
# on a 26.04 developer box with a newer libproxy, and only when SshFetcher's background
# reconnect gets far enough to open a socket. That intermittency is the reason it is
# suppressed by name rather than left to be rediscovered as a flaky red build.
#
# Worth recording, since it is visible here and nowhere else: an ssh:// connect consults
# the DESKTOP'S HTTP proxy configuration, and libproxy may evaluate a PAC script to answer.
# loftail has never had a proxy setting and does not document one. Turning it off for the
# SSH socket (QAbstractSocket::setProxy(QNetworkProxy::NoProxy)) would remove this leak at
# its source and take a synchronous lookup off the connect path — but it would also break
# anyone who reaches their hosts THROUGH a system SOCKS proxy today, so it is a product
# decision and not a sanitizer one. Suppressing the symptom deliberately leaves it open.
leak:px_proxy_factory_new
leak:px_manager_new_with_options
leak:libpxbackend-1.0.so
leak:QNetworkProxyFactory::systemProxyForQuery
